# Architecture

Reference host: Apple Silicon Mac, 64 GB unified memory, macOS. A 48 GB Mac can host with a 40 GB ceiling and `deep-brain` swapping (ADR 0031). oMLX serves
local roles on `127.0.0.1:1234` from `~/.hearth/models` (ADR 0026).
`scripts/omlx.sh down` stops oMLX and leaves the port empty (ADR 0028).
Do not also run headless `llmster`. The gateway era is in `docs/HISTORY.md`.

## Shape

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph onHost["On the host"]
        aider["aider"]
        zed["Zed"]
        scripts["scripts / benches"]
        searx["SearXNG :8888<br/>(Podman)"]
        sbx["sandboxed.sh agents"]
        srv["oMLX 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>main-brain: 256K x 3<br/>deep-brain: 64K, swaps with main-brain<br/>token required"]
    end

    laptop["Laptop<br/>SSH tunnel, own token<br/>optional edit-brain on own oMLX"]
    cloud["Gemini<br/>laptop ask-cloud only, laptop's own key"]

    aider --> srv
    zed --> srv
    scripts --> srv
    zed -.->|"Web and Write + search"| searx
    sbx -->|"only allowed egress"| srv
    laptop -->|"ssh -L, tunnel-only key"| srv
    laptop -.-> cloud

    classDef localCls fill:#E1F5EE,stroke:#0F6E56,color:#085041
    classDef leaveCls fill:#FAEEDA,stroke:#854F0B,color:#633806
    class srv localCls
    class cloud,searx leaveCls
```

SearXNG in that diagram is the host's. A laptop runs its own on
`127.0.0.1:8888`. The tunnel forwards oMLX only (ADR 0045).

Everything addresses **roles**, never model names. Concrete models appear in
exactly two files: `config/roles.tsv` and `docs/MODELS.md`.

## Roles

| Role | Backend | What |
|---|---|---|
| `main-brain` | oMLX | the resident local model. 256K context (262,144), 3 concurrent predictions (ADR 0046) |
| `deep-brain` | oMLX | slower, more careful model for hard one-off questions. Evicts `main-brain` for its session on every tier (ADR 0031, 0033). `ask-deep` unloads it on the way out and `main-brain` is loaded again. Zed and laptops can use it only while the host has it in (`scripts/demand-load.sh deep-brain up`) |
| `edit-brain` | laptop oMLX | editor edit prediction: `/v1/completions` only, 8K × 2, on the laptop's own oMLX. Never a chat or agent model; host does not load it (ADR 0033) |
| `cloud-cheap` | Gemini | client-only: a laptop's `ask-cloud` with that laptop's own key. Never on the host, never in oMLX (ADR 0038) |

`local-code` and `local-chat` were collapsed into `main-brain` (D1): two heavy
models cannot both stay resident with 3 × 256K slots, and evicting the agent
model was failure F2. The old alias names still work and mean `main-brain`.

`scripts/omlx.sh up` loads the resident role from `~/.hearth/models`. A
demand role stays unloaded until `scripts/demand-load.sh` or `ask-deep`.

## The privacy boundary

There is no router and no key scope. The boundary is where models live:

- **oMLX holds only local models.** Anything sent to `:1234` stays on
  the host by construction. Rule: no script adds a cloud backend to
  `config/roles.tsv` except as an explicit cloud row (`gemini`).
- **The host holds no cloud key** (ADR 0038). Only a laptop's `ask-cloud`
  passes one, its own, per invocation. Host Zed has no cloud provider (D3).
- **Sandboxed agents can reach `127.0.0.1:1234` only**, so they cannot reach
  cloud at all.

What this does **not** do, stated plainly: tokens are not scoped to models
(any valid token reaches every loaded model — fine while all are local), and
there is no spend cap or per-request log. A Gemini quota in the Google Cloud
console is the cap.

## Server settings

`scripts/omlx.sh up` writes `~/.omlx/settings.json` (mode 600). No token →
401, wrong token → 401, host token → 200.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Bind | `127.0.0.1:1234` — laptops use an SSH tunnel |
| Authentication | on |
| CORS | off |
| Model fallback | off |
| MCP | off |

## Process supervision

oMLX serves `main-brain` on `127.0.0.1:1234` from `~/.hearth/models` (ADR 0026).
Weights are linked as org/leaf under `~/.omlx/models`; the role is an oMLX
`model_alias` so clients and the community board disagree usefully (ADR 0034).
`ask-deep` loads `deep-brain` from the same tree in its place (ADR 0031, 0033).
Login runs `scripts/boot.sh`, which runs `scripts/omlx.sh up`, then starts
Podman and SearXNG. Open WebUI was removed (ADR 0044). `scripts/omlx.sh down` stops oMLX and
leaves the port empty.

### Environment files

| File | Read by | Contains |
|---|---|---|
| `~/.config/hearth/env` (mode 600) | aliases (`_hearth_var`), scripts | `OMLX_HOST_TOKEN` only. The Google key left the host on 2026-11-08 (ADR 0038); preflight fails if it is back |

The aliases read single values from these files and inject them per command;
they never `source` them into the interactive shell. Do not export a provider
key from `~/.zshenv` or `~/.zshrc`: every process, including local aider and
anything an agent spawns, would then hold it.

## Verification

| Script | Answers | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| `healthcheck.sh` | host token 200 (positive control), no/wrong token 401, each role answers, oMLX on :1234, LAN silent, no CORS, an unknown model name is refused or warned | 0 / 1 / 2 |
| `preflight.sh` | roles.tsv well-formed and local-only on omlx, weights on disk and in MODELS.md, rule 1, oMLX settings, secrets placement, weight drift, autostart, bash 3.2 / CRLF | 0 / 1 / 2 |
| `status.sh` | one glance, no model calls | — |
| `omlx.sh status` | whether oMLX is listening and which roles it reports | 0 / 1 |
| `pin-digests.sh --check [--deep]` | weights unchanged since recorded (D4) | 0 / 1 / 2 |
| `tool-smoke.sh` | Zed-like read → write tool loop | 0 / 1 |
| `concurrency-bench.sh`, `bench.sh` | load behaviour; quality tasks | — |
| `sandbox-probe.sh` | sandboxed agents reach :1234 and nothing else | 0 / 1 |
| `vscode-check.sh` | VS Code settings keep code on the host: no Copilot completions/NES/code search, telemetry off, loopback endpoints only | 0 / 1 / 2 |
| `loop-guard.sh --self-test` | the PreToolUse loop guard denies repeats and stops over budget, in both hook formats | 0 / 1 |

Exit 2 always means "could not run", never "found a problem".

An unknown model name can be answered by the loaded role. Nothing new loads,
but a typo'd model id silently gets `main-brain`. `healthcheck.sh` reports
it as a warning.

## Measured (2026-10-31)

| | Ollama via LiteLLM (phase 0) | llmster, 64K × 3 (phase 1) |
|---|---|---|
| c1 TTFT p95, 28K prompt | 21.0 s | 19.9 s |
| c3 TTFT p95 | 65.8 s | 59.9 s |
| c3 total p50 | 40.3 s | 61.0 s |
| Peak footprint | 37.0 GB (1 slot) | 29.7 GB (3 slots) |

Prefill was still serialised on that MLX engine; `--parallel` batches
decode, so all three requests in a round finish together. The wins are
memory, no evictions and no gateway hop, not concurrency.
`bench/results/2026-10-31-phase0`, `…-phase1`. The live server is the LM
Studio app (D6), loaded at 128K × 3 (ADR 0017). Those rows are the 64K
measurement, not a claim that llmster is still running. The current
`main-brain` (ADR 0030) at the old 128K cap: c3 TTFT p50 60.7 s / p95 61.5 s, c3 total
p95 62.3 s, peak 34.0 GB (`bench/results/2026-11-06-2012-concurrency-c3-qwen3.6`).
The cap is now 262,144 (ADR 0046). The 30k c3 on 2026-11-10 matched that
TTFT (p50 59.8 s / p95 60.4 s). One 200,266-token prompt took 377.6 s and
peaked at 40.0 GB (`bench/results/2026-11-10-262144-main-200k`).
The previous resident the same day was c3 TTFT p50 79.2 s / p95 88.2 s, total
p95 88.9 s, peak 31.0 GB (`bench/results/2026-11-06-1956-concurrency-c3-local-main`).
Earlier, ADR 0024: c1 TTFT p95 25.0 s, c3 total p95 75.2 s, peak 25.2 GB
(`bench/results/2026-11-01-gemma-concurrency`). ADR 0023, same day: c1 TTFT
p95 21.8 s, c3 total p95 65.5 s, peak 29.7 GB (`bench/results/2026-11-01-128k`).

## The second boundary: containment

The local/cloud split governs **where data goes on purpose**. It has no
jurisdiction over a compromised agent — an injected aider that `curl`s a
stolen key to an attacker never touches the model server.

`scripts/sandboxed.sh` closes that channel by denying egress to everything
except `127.0.0.1:1234`. Verified by `scripts/sandbox-probe.sh` (11/11 on
2026-10-31, including a deny check on the old `:4000`). Reads are
deliberately unrestricted; see `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` and ADR 0007.

| Threat | Model placement | Sandbox |
|---|---|---|
| A prompt sends client data to a cloud model | **caught** on the host (no cloud key). On a laptop, unless you run `ask-cloud` | not caught |
| An injected agent exfiltrates a key via curl | not caught | **caught** |

## Several people

Each person has their own brain (ADR 0037). Another person's Mac, mini, Pi,
or NAS does not run this host's prompts, and this host does not run theirs.
oMLX stays on `127.0.0.1:1234`. Laptops still reach it by the SSH tunnel in
`docs/LAPTOP.md`. The LAN-silent check is unchanged. That decision installs
no coordinator, small role, or mail tool.

## Known gaps

- **Host SSH accepts passwords** (macOS default). `docs/LAPTOP.md` gives the
  key-only override; not applied automatically.
- **`scripts/omlx.sh up` rewrites `~/.omlx/settings.json`.** `healthcheck.sh`
  checks the live listener and CORS header.
- **Untrusted MCP servers are unaudited** — `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` §3.
- **Zed's built-in `fetch` and `search_web` tools** reach the internet in the
  Write and Ask profiles; that predates this migration.
# Claude Project spec

Paste the two fields below into claude.ai → Projects → New project.
Keep this file as the source of truth; when the instructions change here, re-paste.

---

## Title

```
Hearth — Local + Cloud
```

## Description

```
Private-first local inference on a Mac Studio. oMLX serves local
roles on 127.0.0.1:1234. One cloud path, ask-cloud, calls Gemini with its own
key. Routing rules, the model registry, and the clients (Zed,
shell aliases). Roles are re-pointed without rewriting clients.
```

---

## Custom instructions

```
# Context

Solo developer. Local inference on this Mac; one cloud caller for work that
is not sensitive. Uses Zed. Laptops reach the host through an
SSH tunnel, not the LAN.

This project covers the AI stack itself — not the applications built on it.
Source of truth is the hearth repo. Current design: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.

# Architecture (assume this unless I say otherwise)

oMLX serves on 127.0.0.1:1234. Clients talk OpenAI-compatible
to that port and name a role. There is no gateway and no LAN bind. ask-cloud
is the only path that calls a cloud API, and it calls Gemini directly.

Models are addressed by ROLE, never by vendor model name:

  main-brain               the resident local model
  deep-brain              slow, careful local model; loaded per ask-deep session
  local-code              alias of main-brain
  local-chat              alias of main-brain
  cloud-cheap             Gemini, a laptop's ask-cloud only (host has no key) — bulk non-sensitive work

cloud-code, cloud-frontier, cloud-frontier-direct, and cloud-frontier-fast are dormant: no keys.

Concrete model names are written by hand in exactly two places:
config/roles.tsv and docs/MODELS.md. Nowhere else. Generated copies
(Zed display labels, pins/digests.tsv) are written by scripts from roles.tsv.

# Rules

1. Never hardcode a vendor model name outside config/roles.tsv and
   docs/MODELS.md. Aliases, scripts, IDE settings and code reference roles only.
   If you catch yourself writing a model tag in a shell alias, stop.

2. Privacy is structural, not advisory. Sensitive private data, PII, any repo I have
   flagged as holding real data, and anything else I mark sensitive: local
   roles only. oMLX holds only local models, and only ask-cloud carries
   a cloud key. If you are writing code or config that could send that class
   of data to a cloud endpoint, refuse and say why. Never add a cloud provider
   to host Zed.

3. Treat your own knowledge of models as stale. The field moves faster than your
   training data. Before asserting a price, a context window, a benchmark result,
   or that a model exists, search — and give the date of what you found. Do not
   recommend from memory.

4. State the cost tier whenever you propose a cloud model, in $/M input and
   $/M output, with the source. Cheap local work should stay local; say so when
   I am about to spend money on something a 30B model would handle.

5. Nothing replaces a role until it has been benched. New model claims get run
   against bench/tasks/ and compared to the incumbent before the config changes.

6. Routing and model-choice changes get an ADR in docs/decisions/ — short: context,
   decision, consequences, date. I already work this way.

7. Shell scripts: portable bash that runs under zsh on macOS. LF line endings only,
   never CRLF — it breaks launchd and shebangs. Applies to .sh, .plist, configs.
   macOS /bin/bash is 3.2: no declare -A, no mapfile, no ${x^^}, and guard empty
   arrays as ${arr[@]+"${arr[@]}"} under set -u. Code that only works on bash 4
   passes every test I can run and then dies on the target machine.

7a. Every diagram is Mermaid. No ASCII art, no committed SVG or PNG. GitHub
   renders Mermaid natively, it diffs as text, and it cannot drift out of sync
   with the prose the way an exported image does. If something genuinely cannot
   be expressed in Mermaid, use a Markdown table.

8. Work in this order: plan → I refine → I approve → build. Do not start building
   because a plan looks obviously right. Show me the plan first.

9. Be concise and direct. No preamble, no restating my question back at me, no
   summarising what you just did at length. If you can cut a sentence and keep the
   meaning, cut it.

10. When something is uncertain, say so plainly and tell me the cheapest way to
    find out. Do not fill gaps with confident-sounding guesses.

# Repo layout

  <hearth-repo>/
    CLAUDE.md              rules for agents working in this repo
    AGENTS.md              shared agent rules (Zed, aider read this too)
    docs/
      ARCHITECTURE.md      how serving is wired
      ROUTING.md           the decision table: what goes local vs cloud
      MODELS.md            registry — role → model, date set, why
      RUNBOOK.md           start, stop, diagnose
      decisions/           ADRs
    config/roles.tsv       role → model key, context, slots
    scripts/               load, healthcheck, bench, sandbox
    bench/tasks/           fixed prompts for comparing models

# Hardware envelope

64 GB unified, but macOS caps GPU-accessible memory at ~48 GB (75%).
main-brain is one model at 262144 × 3 slots (ADR 0046); deep-brain is 65536 and swapped in for it on demand (ADR 0020).
A second heavy model does not fit beside it. Say when a suggestion won't fit.
```

