Do **not** attach anything containing API keys.
# How this compares to other private-AI setups

> **Historical (gateway era).** Written for the LiteLLM + Ollama stack; superseded by ADR 0016. See `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` for the current design.

Read 2026-08-02. Two reference points worth knowing about: one person solving
the same problem from a different angle, and one product solving the piece we
found most annoying.

---

## Vitalik Buterin — "My self-sovereign / local / private / secure LLM setup"

[April 2026](https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2026/04/02/secure_llms.html).
Near-identical brief: *"what kind of AI setup would we build if we took privacy,
security and self-sovereignty as non-negotiable?"*

### Where we independently landed on the same thing

- **SearXNG** for search, for the same reason — reduce what any single engine
  learns about you.
- **AGENTS.md** as the shared instruction file.
- **Local-first with a deliberate cloud escape hatch.** He hit the same wall we
  did: local models are fine on well-trodden ground and fall apart on hard
  problems. He tried and failed to get Qwen3.5:35B to implement BLS-12-381
  hash-to-point, and sent it to Claude, which one-shotted it. Our `cloud-code`
  role exists for exactly that moment.
- **Roughly the same speed floor.** He says "anything slower than 50 tok/sec
  feels too annoying to be worth it." Our roles run 65–120.

### Where he is ahead — and these are real gaps for us

**1. Sandboxing — closed, after this was written.**

He runs LLM work inside [bubblewrap](https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap):
`sbox` in any directory creates a sandbox rooted there, and anything started
inside can only see that directory plus explicit whitelists. Ports, audio and
filesystem are all constrained.

We had nothing equivalent when this file was first written: `aider` and Zed's
agent ran with full user permissions, able to read `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, every
repo on the machine, and the gateway's own `.env`. `scripts/sandboxed.sh` and
ADR 0007 answered that — an SBPL profile that restricts capability rather than
attempting to detect injection. Different mechanism from bubblewrap, same
instinct.

**2. A threat model we never wrote down: prompt injection.**

He cites research that roughly **15% of published agent skills contained
malicious instructions**, and a demonstration where summarising a web page
caused an agent to download and execute a shell script. Our stack has SearXNG
wired into a chat UI and agents that read repositories. Both are injection
surfaces. We have thought hard about where data *goes* and not at all about what
untrusted content might *make the agent do*.

**3. Human confirmation as a firewall.**

His messaging daemon can read messages and send only to himself autonomously.
Sending to anyone else requires explicit confirmation. His framing is the one
worth stealing:

> the new "two-factor confirmation" is that the two factors are the human and
> the LLM ... the hope is that humans and LLMs fail in distinct ways

We have `--no-auto-commits` and Zed's tool-restricted profiles, which are the
same instinct applied only to code. Nothing gates an action with consequences.

**4. A local `world_knowledge` corpus.**

A Wikipedia dump plus manuals for things he cares about, so questions answerable
locally never reach a search engine. For a regulated domain that would be
reference docs and internal playbooks — the queries most worth not leaking. Our `local-embed` role
exists and indexes nothing.

### Where we are ahead

**Multi-machine access.** He dismisses the DGX Spark partly because *"you have to
figure out the networking details of how to connect to it from your actual work
device"*. That is precisely what our gateway plus scoped keys plus client shim
solves. His setup is one-machine; ours serves laptops with per-device keys,
spend caps and revocation.

**Structural privacy at a different layer.** His isolation is process-level
(sandbox). Ours is route-level (key scope). They fail differently and are
complementary rather than competing:

| Threat | Sandbox | Key scope |
|---|---|---|
| Agent reads a file it shouldn't | **caught** | not caught |
| Prompt sends client data to a cloud model | not caught | **caught** |
| Compromised client machine | partly | **caught** — key is scoped |
| Malicious skill exfiltrates via curl | **caught** | not caught |

We have one column. He has the other. Having both is the correct answer.

**Measurement.** He reports tok/s per hardware. We bench roles on real tasks and
record the decision. His post is explicitly a starting point, not a finished
product — he says so in a warning at the top.

**Model indirection.** He swaps models by hand through llama-swap; we address
roles, so nothing downstream changes.

### One tooling disagreement worth noting

He moved from Ollama to `llama-server` + `llama-swap`, because **Ollama could
not fit Qwen3.5:35B on his GPU and llama-server could**. That is a real
efficiency difference, not tribal preference. On 64 GB unified we are not
memory-pressed at 37.5 GB resident, so it has not bitten us — but if a future
role does not fit, that is the first thing to try before buying hardware.

---

## Routerly — the database-free gateway

[github.com/Inebrio/Routerly](https://github.com/Inebrio/Routerly), AGPL-3.0.
Directly addresses the thing we found most irritating.

### What it does that LiteLLM does not

| | Routerly | LiteLLM (ours) |
|---|---|---|
| Database | **none** — JSON in `~/.routerly/` | Postgres + Prisma, 64 tables |
| Native Anthropic `/v1/messages` | **yes** | no — translates to OpenAI format |
| Per-project tokens + budgets | yes | yes (virtual keys) |
| Per-project model list | yes | yes |
| Routing policies | 9, including LLM-picks-the-model | static fallbacks |
| Provider breadth | 8 | 100+ |
| MCP gateway | **none** | yes — scoped by key, team, org |
| Maturity | v0.1.5, 43 stars, 3 contributors | widely deployed |

**Checked again 2026-08-02: Routerly still has no MCP support.** That now cuts
against switching. LiteLLM's MCP gateway applies the same key scoping to tools
as to models, which is what ADR 0008 is built on — leaving would mean rebuilding
tool access control as well as the routing verification below.

**The interesting unlock is native Anthropic format.** Claude Code is currently a
documented exception in our stack because LiteLLM only speaks OpenAI shape.
Routerly handles `/v1/messages` natively, so Claude Code could route through the
gateway and gain spend tracking and logging. That is a real capability, not a
tidiness argument.

### Why we are not switching yet

- **v0.1.5, one release, three contributors, 43 stars.** This is pre-1.0
  software that would hold our provider keys and enforce our privacy boundary.
  LiteLLM's 64 tables are ugly; ugly is not the same as risky.
- **The cost of switching is the verification, not the config.** The leak test,
  fallback audit, digest pinning and every script touching `/key/*` would need
  redoing and re-proving. That is the expensive part, and it buys removing
  something that currently runs in a container we never look at.
- **AGPL plus a commercial-licensing address** is an open-core shape. Worth
  watching whether the free tier narrows.

Revisit at 1.0, or the first time Postgres actually causes a problem rather
than merely offending.

---

## What we should actually do about this

In rough priority. Items 1 and 2 are now done; the rest stand.

1. ~~**Sandboxing for agent work.**~~ Done — `scripts/sandboxed.sh`, ADR 0007.
2. ~~**Write down a prompt-injection threat model.**~~ Partly done. ADR 0007
   covers agent containment and ADR 0008 covers tool scope. Web search feeding
   the chat UI is still unmodelled.
3. **Confirmation gates for consequential actions.** Currently only `git commit`
   qualifies, which is why this has not hurt. It would matter the moment
   anything in this stack can send an email or move money.
4. **`world_knowledge` corpus.** Domain reference material and internal
   playbooks indexed locally via `local-embed`, so the queries most worth not
   leaking never reach SearXNG.
5. **Note llama-server** as the first thing to try if a model does not fit,
   before assuming the hardware is the limit.

The honest summary as first written: **we built the routing and governance
layer well, and the isolation layer not at all.** Vitalik built the isolation
layer and skipped the routing. ADR 0007 closed most of our side of that gap,
and ADR 0008 extended the governance layer to cover tools as well as models —
which was a hole neither of us had named.
# Distributing Hearth across hosts — draft plan

**Status:** draft for operator review, revised 2026-11-08. Nothing here is
built or approved. Each phase that lands gets its own ADR (rule 8). No model
names here (rule 1): candidates go into `docs/MODELS.md` once benched.

## Decided so far (operator, 2026-11-08)

- Nodes run **llama.cpp** (`llama-server`). oMLX stays the host's only engine.
- Nodes do **background batch work with small models**: never interactive,
  never in Zed's or aider's path.
- Hearth needs a **node registry** and a way to run and watch nodes.

## Why not more

None of the old boxes can serve `main-brain` or `deep-brain`. Splitting one
model across nodes (llama.cpp RPC, exo) is slower than one box whenever the
model fits on one, and llama.cpp RPC has no authentication. Rejected.

Expected speeds come from web research (2026-11-08) and are unbenched:
Intel Mac mini 2018 at about 4–8 t/s for 4B Q4 (estimated from memory
bandwidth), Pi 5 at about 2–6 t/s for 3B Q4. Prefill is slow, so a job with
a long input takes minutes. That is fine in a queue and useless in an editor.

## Shape

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph host["Host: control plane"]
        reg["config/nodes.tsv<br/>config/roles.tsv"]
        nodes["scripts/nodes.sh<br/>up / down / status / pin-check"]
        q["~/.hearth/jobs<br/>queue / running / done / failed"]
        worker["scripts/batch.sh work<br/>(launchd)"]
        tun["tunnels 127.0.0.1:12xx<br/>(launchd)"]
        srv["oMLX 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>unchanged"]
    end
    subgraph n1["Node: Intel mini"]
        c1["sshd: control key<br/>forced node.sh"]
        l1["llama-server 127.0.0.1:8080"]
    end
    subgraph n2["Node: Pi"]
        c2["sshd: control key<br/>forced node.sh"]
        l2["llama-server 127.0.0.1:8080"]
    end
    reg --> nodes
    reg --> worker
    q --> worker
    worker --> tun
    tun -->|"ssh -L"| l1
    tun -->|"ssh -L"| l2
    nodes -->|"ssh"| c1
    nodes -->|"ssh"| c2

    classDef localCls fill:#E1F5EE,stroke:#0F6E56,color:#085041
    class srv,l1,l2 localCls
```

The host is the whole control plane. Nodes run only `llama-server` and sshd.
They never call the host, and there is no daemon or agent of Hearth's own on
them. That keeps a node as dumb as possible and the attack surface where it
already is.

## 1. Registry

**`config/nodes.tsv`** (new; holds no model names):

| Column | Example | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `node` | `mini` | short name, used in keys and logs |
| `ssh` | `hearth-mini` | `~/.ssh/config` alias on the host |
| `os` | `macos` / `linux` | which service manager `node.sh` uses |
| `ram_gb` | `16` | picks the role row, like `min_ram` on the host |
| `trust` | `local` / `public-only` | `local` = operator owns it, disk encrypted, operator's network. Anything else is `public-only` |
| `port` | `1241` | host-side tunnel port for this node's llama-server |
| `state` | `active` / `drained` | `drained` = worker sends nothing new |

**`config/roles.tsv`** gains a role with `backend` `llamacpp` and a tenth
column, `nodes` (comma-separated, or `-` for host rows). Example:
`local-batch llamacpp <model_key> 8192 2 <weights.gguf> - resident 8 mini,pi`.
`min_ram` is checked against each node's `ram_gb`. `preflight.sh` fails
if a `llamacpp` row names an unknown node, or if any `omlx` row names a node.

Several nodes may serve one role only if they run **the same pinned weights**
and share **the same trust**. Then spreading jobs between them is load
balancing, not a fallback.

## 2. Control plane

**Keys.** Each node gets two host-side keys, both restricted in the node's
`authorized_keys`:

- *tunnel key*: `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen="127.0.0.1:8080"`
  (the `docs/LAPTOP.md` pattern, the other way round).
- *control key*: `command="~/hearth-node/node.sh --ssh",restrict`. `node.sh`
  reads `SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND` and accepts only `up`, `down`, `status`,
  `pin-check`, `logs`. No shell.

**`node/node.sh`** (new top-level `node/` directory, like `client/`; not in
`scripts/`, which stays host-only) runs on the node. It manages llama-server
under launchd (mini) or systemd (Linux), binds `127.0.0.1` only, sets
`--api-key` from a mode-600 file, `--metrics`, `--slots` off (slot state can
contain prompts), and turns off logging of prompts. It is bash 3.2-safe so one
copy works on both.

**`scripts/nodes.sh`** (host):

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `nodes.sh status` | per node: ssh reachable, tunnel up, `/health`, 401 without token, loopback-only listener, loaded weights, metrics (tokens/s, requests), queue depth |
| `nodes.sh up/down <node>` | `node.sh up/down` over the control key |
| `nodes.sh push <node>` | copies pinned GGUF from host `~/.hearth/models` after a sha256 check. The node never downloads weights itself |
| `nodes.sh pin-check` | node hashes its GGUF and the host compares with `pins/digests.tsv` (rule 6) |
| `nodes.sh drain/resume <node>` | flips `state` |

`healthcheck.sh`, `preflight.sh` and `status.sh` call into `nodes.sh status`.

**Tunnels.** One launchd agent on the host (alongside the existing
`com.hearth.tunnel`) keeps `ssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:<port>:127.0.0.1:8080` up per
active node.

**Dashboard.** Phase A is a CLI: `nodes.sh status` and `batch.sh status`.
If you want a page later, `status.sh --html` writes a static page under
`~/.hearth/` (mode 600) that you open locally. It is generated, has no server
and no write actions. A web control plane with buttons is its own decision,
because it would be a new listener that can start and stop things.

## 3. Batch queue

**Submitting** (host):

```bash
scripts/batch.sh submit local-batch --template prompts/tag.txt docs/inbox/*.md
scripts/batch.sh submit local-batch --public --jsonl items.jsonl
```

Each input becomes `~/.hearth/jobs/queue/<id>.json` (dir 700, files 600)
holding role, template, input path, `max_tokens`, `sensitivity`. Sensitivity
defaults to **private**. `--public` is explicit, the same stance as `ask-cloud`.

**Worker:** `scripts/batch.sh work` runs under launchd, in Python as other scripts already do.

- Picks each role's active nodes from the registry. Keeps at most `slots`
  jobs in flight per node (llama-server answers 503 or "no slot" rather than
  queueing, so the worker does the queueing).
- `queue → running → done | failed`, using an atomic `mv`. Output is written
  next to the job. On start, jobs stuck in `running` past a timeout go back
  to the queue.
- Retries twice with backoff on a connection error or 503, on the same role.
  It **never** reroutes to `main-brain`, another role or cloud. A role with
  no healthy node leaves its jobs queued, and `batch.sh status` says so.
- Refuses to send a private job to a `public-only` node. Checked at submit
  and again at dispatch.

**Not in v1:** agents submitting jobs. Sandboxed agents keep reaching
`127.0.0.1:1234` only, so the sandbox is not widened (`docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`
§5). Allowing it later is its own ADR.

**Batch jobs that fit:** tagging and classifying documents, short summaries,
extracting fields to JSON (llama.cpp grammar / JSON schema), renaming
files, and embeddings for a document index if a caller appears (ADR 0018).

## 4. First job: document tagging (operator pick, 2026-11-08)

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    src["source folder<br/>(read-only)"] --> ext["extract on host<br/>pdftotext / textutil / plain"]
    ext -->|"no text"| ocr["needs-ocr list<br/>(not sent)"]
    ext --> trim["trim: filename + first ~3K tokens"]
    trim --> q["batch queue<br/>private"]
    q --> node["local-batch on a local-trust node<br/>JSON schema output"]
    node --> idx["~/.hearth/tags/index.tsv"]
    idx -.->|"opt-in"| finder["Finder tags (xattr)"]
```

`scripts/tag-docs.sh <folder>` submits a folder to `batch.sh`. That is the
whole job interface.

| Step | Design |
|---|---|
| Extract | On the host, with tools already installed: `pdftotext -layout` for PDF, `textutil -convert txt` for doc/docx/rtf, as-is for md/txt. A PDF with no text layer goes on a `needs-ocr` list and is not sent. OCR is a later job |
| Trim | Filename + page count + the first ~3K tokens. Prefill is the slow part on CPU, so the cap gets tuned in Phase 0 to a per-document time target (proposed ≤ 60 s on the mini) |
| Vocabulary | Closed set in `config/tags.tsv` (`tag`, one-line description), plus `other`. A small model picks from a list far more reliably than it invents tags |
| Output | Constrained by llama-server's JSON-schema support (verify on the Phase 0 build): `{"doc_type": <enum>, "tags": [<enum>, ≤3], "confidence": "low"\|"medium"\|"high"}`. No free-text summary in v1: decode is slow, and free text is where small models drift |
| Store | `~/.hearth/tags/index.tsv` (mode 600): path, file sha256, doc_type, tags, confidence, role, weights digest, date. Source files are **never modified**. Finder tags are an opt-in `--finder` flag that writes only the xattr |
| Re-runs | Skip a file whose sha256 and weights digest both match the index. A new model re-tags everything; a changed file re-tags itself |
| Low confidence | `low` rows are listed by `tag-docs.sh review` for a person to check. They are not dropped |
| Privacy | Jobs are private, so only `trust=local` nodes. Extracted text lives in the job files (mode 600) and is deleted when the job reaches `done` |

**Gate (rule 5):** a new `bench/tasks/05-tagging/` with about 30 labelled
documents. The committed set is **synthetic or public only**. If real
documents are used to check accuracy, their labels and paths stay outside git.
Score: doc_type accuracy and tag F1 per candidate model, `main-brain` on the
host as the reference ceiling, plus seconds per document on each node. The
operator sets pass marks after seeing the first run.

## Rules this plan keeps

| Rule | How |
|---|---|
| 2: `:1234` local-only | untouched. Node roles have their own backend and ports |
| 3: privacy by placement | `trust` in the registry and private-by-default jobs. A VPS is cloud and never gets a `local` row |
| 5: bench first | no `llamacpp` row before Phase 0 numbers for that node class |
| 6: drift | `nodes.sh pin-check`. As on the host, llama-server loads whatever is on disk; drift is detected, not prevented |
| 7: RAM | per node, through `ram_gb` and `min_ram` |
| 9: bash 3.2 | `node.sh`, `nodes.sh`. Worker logic in Python |
| No fallbacks | jobs wait. They are never rerouted |

## Phases

| Phase | Work | Gate |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | `llama-bench -ngl 0` plus one `bench/tasks`-style batch task on each box (1B / 3–4B / 8B Q4), recording build commit and threads | numbers in `docs/MODELS.md` *Candidates*. Go/no-go per box |
| A | `nodes.tsv`, `node/node.sh`, `scripts/nodes.sh`, keys, tunnels, checks. One node, one role | `nodes.sh status` all green. Checks see 401 without a token and nothing on the LAN |
| B | `batch.sh` queue and worker | 100-job run: none lost, none duplicated, a node pulled mid-run leaves jobs queued and rerouted nowhere |
| C | second node, and SearXNG moved to a node (optional) | — |
| D | off-LAN node over Tailscale/Headscale: grants `tag:hearth-node` `tcp:22` only, SSH on top, no port forwards | ADR |

## Risks

- **Intel mini end of life:** Sequoia is its last macOS. Once its security
  updates stop, drop it to `public-only` or move it to Linux.
- **Pi disk:** an unencrypted SD card. Encrypt it with LUKS or mark the node
  `public-only`. Keep prompt logging off.
- **Upkeep:** every node adds two keys, a service, a tunnel and a drift
  check. Start with one node.

## Open questions

1. Which folder or folders, and which file types (PDF, docx, email, scans)?
   Roughly how many documents, and how many new ones a week?
2. The tag vocabulary: do you already have categories (document types,
   matter stages, …), or should a first pass with `main-brain` over a sample
   propose a list for you to edit?
3. Index only, or also Finder tags?
4. Exact boxes: Pi model and RAM, any old PC with a GPU?
5. Static HTML status page in Phase A, or CLI only?

## Sources (searched 2026-11-08)

- Pi 5 speeds: specpicks.com Pi 5 16 GB review; bestllmfor.com
- Old x86: mgroeber.de "Benchmarking llama.cpp on legacy hardware"
- Split inference: localaimaster.com distributed inference
- llama-server router mode, `--api-key`, public `/health`, `/metrics`, `/slots` holds prompts: glukhov.org router mode; mvysny.github.io llama-server endpoints
- Slots and 503 on full: dev.to "slot unavailable under load"; llama.cpp server README
- macOS support: mac.install.guide compatibility
- Tailscale grants: tailscale.com/kb/1324/acl-grants
# What we have tried

A running record of serving-stack and role experiments: what was tried, what
it measured, and why it stayed or went. Newest last. Add a row whenever an
ADR lands or a bench run changes a decision.

Model names stay out of this file (hard rule 1) — which weights each role
used is in `docs/MODELS.md` (Live / Retired). Raw numbers are in
`bench/results/<run>/`, committed since 2026-10-31. Runs before that were
re-checked in and had domain wording in old task prompts and model answers
redacted (billing/invoice substituted); figures are unchanged.

## Serving stack

| Date | Tried | Result | Outcome | Record |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-08-01 | Inherited setup: Ollama + LiteLLM, ad-hoc model names | Worked, no role discipline, no privacy boundary | Rebuilt around roles and scoped keys | ADR 0001 |
| 2026-08-02 | Postgres + Prisma for LiteLLM virtual keys | Per-key scope; leak test passes | Kept until 0016 | ADR 0002 |
| 2026-08-02 | Ollama bound to localhost; LAN via gateway only | LAN probe unreachable | Kept until 0016 | ADR 0003 |
| 2026-08-02 | Agents under `sandboxed.sh`, egress only to the gateway | Probe proves nothing else reachable | Kept; target port moves to `:1234` | ADR 0007 |
| 2026-08-02 | MCP tools through the gateway, deny by default, scoped by key | Works; adds gateway coupling | Superseded by 0016 (MCP moves to the client) | ADR 0008 |
| 2026-09-04 | Context capped at 64K for local roles | Fits the RAM envelope | Kept | ADR 0010 |
| 2026-09-04 | Ollama memory knobs: flash attention, q8_0 KV, 1 loaded model, 1 parallel | One slot for every client; any role switch evicts the agent model | Superseded by 0016 (F1, F2) | ADR 0011 |
| 2026-09-06 | `local-big` on Rapid-MLX `:8000` | Tool smoke 2.6 s vs 12–18 s on Ollama | Superseded by 0013 (second server to supervise) | ADR 0012, `2026-09-06-1229-tool-smoke` |
| 2026-10-22 | `local-big` on Ollama's MLX runner, one inference server | ~111 tok/s; Zed agent loops still stall under load | Superseded by 0016 | ADR 0013, `2026-10-22-0816` |
| 2026-10-31 | Stall handling: 90 s timeout, no retries, `mlx-watchdog` kills a stuck runner | Treats the symptom (F7) | Superseded by 0016 | ADR 0015 |
| 2026-10-31 | Phase 0 concurrency baseline on that stack | c1 TTFT p95 21.0 s; c3 p95 65.8 s, strictly serialised; no failures, peak 37 GB | Numbers to beat | `2026-10-31-phase0` |
| 2026-10-31 | D6 switched to the LM Studio app owning the server | llmster and the app cannot share `~/.lmstudio`; app costs ~1.1 GB | Kept | ADR 0016 |
